# [SSHM](../wg/sshm.html)

## Summary

The SSH Maintenance (SSHM) Working Group met at IETF 127. The session was co-chaired by Job Snijders (in person) and Stephen Farrell (virtually). The group reviewed the active document pipeline, planned Working Group Last Calls (WGLCs), discussed compression side-channel vulnerabilities in the SSH transport protocol, and addressed questions around post-quantum cryptography and CNSA 2.0 compliance.

## Key Discussion Points

### Working Group Document Status and Pipeline
The chairs presented the [Chair Slides](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-sshm-chair-slides-01) and celebrated the publication of RFC 10042 (hybrid key exchange using ML-KEM). Progression plans for active drafts were established:

- **`draft-ietf-sshm-strict-kex`**: Damien Miller noted that the specification is complete and strictly required for protocol robustness. The chairs agreed to initiate a 3-week Working Group Last Call.
- **`draft-ietf-sshm-chacha20-poly1305`**: The document requires normative text making strict key exchange mandatory when using ChaCha20-Poly1305. Job Snijders and Damien Miller will coordinate on the required text, and a short WGLC will follow the completion of the `draft-ietf-sshm-strict-kex` last call.
- **`draft-ietf-sshm-cert`**: Damien Miller reported that the document is ready and requested feedback on the test vectors. A WGLC will be scheduled following the ChaCha20-Poly1305 draft.
- **`draft-ietf-sshm-hostkey-update`**: The document is currently expired. Damien Miller agreed to re-submit an updated draft. The draft includes an implementation section listing existing implementations as required by the charter, and it will be queued for WGLC after the preceding documents.
- **`draft-ietf-sshm-sshsig-format`**: Recently adopted as a working group document, and a new revision has been submitted. Damien Miller called for reviews and text proposals regarding strong unforgeability properties.
- **`draft-ietf-sshm-composite-sigs`**: Damien Miller noted that test vectors for the ML-DSA-87 / P-384 variant will be updated, and text regarding strong unforgeability needs to be integrated.

### Compression Side-Channel Vulnerabilities in SSH
Job Snijders summarized the interim discussion on cross-channel compression side-channel attacks resulting from a shared LZ77 dictionary across multiple SSH channels (e.g., injecting chosen plaintext into one channel via a SOCKS proxy to leak plaintext from another channel).

- **Research and Risks**: Tom Wiggers and Rich Salz referenced recent cryptographic research (including work by Kenny Patterson and Fabian Bäumer) demonstrating refined compression side-channel techniques.
- **Mitigation Strategies**: Damien Miller explained that OpenSSH mitigates the attack by disabling the LZ77 dictionary aspect (leaving only Huffman coding via zlib without a dictionary) when compression is enabled. He advocated moving compression up to the application protocol layer (e.g., SFTP compression extensions or compressed channel types) rather than keeping it in the SSH transport layer.
- **Protocol Options**: Tero Kivinen noted that compression remains valuable for bandwidth-constrained, single-channel use cases (such as single SFTP sessions) and suggested addressing risks via implementation-level warnings or single-channel restrictions. Paul Wouters noted that IPsec is removing IPComp due to similar side-channel risks (e.g., CRIME/BREACH) and supported relying on application-level tools (such as `rsync -z`).
- **Draft Proposal**: Rich Salz offered to collaborate with academic researchers (Fabian Bäumer, Kenny Patterson) to author an Internet-Draft addressing the removal or safe handling of SSH transport compression.

### Post-Quantum Cryptography and CNSA 2.0 Compliance
- Ahmed Darwish inquired about pure post-quantum (PQ) SSH key exchange compliance (e.g., CNSA 2.0 requirements) compared to hybrid schemes.
- Stephen Farrell, Damien Miller, and Scott Fluhrer clarified that hybrid PQ key exchange can satisfy FIPS requirements when using approved algorithms, while pure-PQ protection profiles for CNSA 2.0 compliance are being handled via the Independent Submission Stream (ISE) rather than within the SSHM Working Group.

